# Backend Standards

## Fastify Routes

- Routes registered as `FastifyPluginAsync` in `src/routes/` grouped by domain
- Request body, params, and query validated with Zod via `fastify-type-provider-zod`
- Business logic lives in `src/services/` — never inline in route handlers

```typescript
// routes/characters/index.ts
const characterRoutes: FastifyPluginAsync = async (app) => {
  app.post('/', {
    schema: { body: CharacterCreateSchema },
    handler: async (req, reply) => {
      const character = await characterService.create(req.user.id, req.body);
      return reply.code(201).send(character);
    },
  });
};
```

## Service Layer

- Services are plain async functions, not classes
- No Fastify types in service files — they are framework-agnostic
- Services accept and return types from `packages/shared`

## Drizzle ORM

- Schema in `src/db/schema/` — one file per domain
- JSONB columns typed with `.$type<T>()` using types from `packages/shared`
- Migrations generated with `drizzle-kit generate` — never written by hand
- No raw SQL unless a specific optimisation requires it

```typescript
// db/schema/characters.ts
export const characters = pgTable('characters', {
  id: uuid('id').primaryKey().defaultRandom(),
  abilityScores: jsonb('ability_scores').$type<AbilityScores>().notNull(),
});
```

## Error Handling

- Use `AppError` class with `statusCode` and `code` fields — never inline `reply.code().send()`
- Never swallow errors silently
- All async handlers use `try/catch`; unexpected errors bubble to Fastify's error handler

```typescript
// ✅ Good
throw new AppError('Character not found', { statusCode: 404, code: 'CHARACTER_NOT_FOUND' });
```

## Authentication

- JWT access token (15 min), refresh token (7 days) in `httpOnly` + `Secure` + `SameSite=Strict` cookie
- Protected routes declare `preHandler: [app.authenticate]`
- `request.user` typed as `JwtPayload` from `packages/shared`

## Database Conventions

- UUID primary keys with `defaultRandom()`
- `created_at` and `updated_at` on all mutable tables
- SRD tables are read-only at the application level — no update/delete routes
- All migrations committed to git in `src/db/migrations/`
